#[doc = r#"This message specifies the planned kinematic details of a particular System's orbit; "orbit" is defined as a series of orbital element sets, ephemeris, vector covariance and/or explicit maneuvers between them.  The message is intended for Systems such as spacecraft whose kinematic plans are best represented in inertial/orbital frames.  Orbit planning can be done manually by operators, by a route planning service or a combination.  This message decouples kinematic planning from allocation, action and other types of planning; the kinematic OrbitPlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans.  It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on route planning.
An OrbitPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes.  For example a coarse OrbitPlan created by the top System in a hierarchical System of System for overall Mission orchestration and deconfliction could be an input to a orbit planning service that the produces a detailed, executable OrbitPlan for a specific subordinate System.
A single System can have multiple independent OrbitPlans for different orbit planning considerations such as orbit station keeping, orbital maneuvers and attitude adjustment.  The different planning considerations can be handled by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture at different times then independently updated and approved.  Optionally, all kinematic orbit planning considerations for a System can be included in a single OrbitPlan.
An OrbitPlan can be created discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans.  An OrbitPlan can only be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan.  A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution order of multiple OrbitPlans and *Plans in general.  An OrbitPlan can be subject to approval per Approval* messages.  Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for an OrbitPlan are given in other OrbitPlan* messages.UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1."#]
