#[doc = r#"This message specifies a set of Requirements that a supporting System has planned in support of another System.  It is intended for peer-to-peer (P2P) C2 use cases where the peers are under different C2 authorities, hierarchies, domains, combatant commands, military services, etc.  It is also intended for use cases where the peers have different opsec, security or classification levels for their operations.

This message enables a supporting System to communicate planned support to a peer System without the full details and complexity from  "primary" *Plan messages such as TaskPlan, RoutePlan and RouteActivityPlan.  Using the SupportPlan message, the P2P C2 process flow between Systems can be decoupled from their primary C2 process flows.  With planned support described in this message, the peer Systems can churn with multiple primary planning iterations (resulting in other *Plan types) without changing the previously coordinated support in the SupportPlan.  A previously coordinated SupportPlan can even be used as a constraint on the primary C2 process flow in the peer Systems. 

The supporting System's primary *Plan messages can include the superset of all Requirements from the primary C2 authority plus multiple supported Systems.  The primary *Plan messages can have full details at the System's highest classification level.  The supporting System's SupportPlan message can include the subset of Requirements that are applicable to a given supported System. 

For example, consider an air domain System at one classification level requesting support from a space domain System at a different classification level in a different combatant command.  The air domain System can request support, at a common classification level, using  SupportPlanRequest* messages addressed to the space domain System.  The space domain System then integrates the requested support Requirements into its primary *Plans.  The primary *Plans can then be reviewed and approved.  Then the subset of Requirements and details relevant to support of the air domain System can be included in a SupportPlan at the common classification level.  The SupportPlan can then be reviewed, approved and activated by the peers.UCI_PRIMITIVE: Data-1."#]
